Subject: [PATCH] allow several restriction enzymes in --cutter

wf-pore-c passes --cutter straight to `pore-c-py digest`, which accepts a
single enzyme. Protocols using two or more (DpnII + NlaIII, or Arima's
DpnII + HinfI) therefore leave the junctions of the second enzyme undigested:
the affected monomer spans two loci, aligns to one, and the rest is
soft-clipped away. The contact is lost silently.

This swaps the digest for `pore-c-aqb digest`, which takes a comma-separated
list and cuts at the union of the sites, and documents the list form in the
schema. With a single enzyme the output is byte-identical to before.

Note that `digest_align_annotate` calls the digest twice - once per chunking
branch, and with the positional arguments in a different order each time
(`digest "$cutter"` reading stdin, and `digest concatemers.bam "$cutter"`).
Both are patched, and pore-c-aqb accepts either order.

Requires pore-c-aqb in the process container:
    pip install git+https://github.com/AQB/wf-pore-c_AQB.git

Generated against epi2me-labs/wf-pore-c master; verified with `git apply`.

---
diff --git a/modules/local/pore-c.nf b/modules/local/pore-c.nf
index b545cde..8709cfe 100644
--- a/modules/local/pore-c.nf
+++ b/modules/local/pore-c.nf
@@ -62,7 +62,7 @@ process digest_align_annotate {
             """
             echo "${chunk_ref}"
             bamindex fetch --chunk=${chunk_index} "concatemers.bam" |
-                pore-c-py digest "${meta.cutter}" --max_monomers ${params.max_monomers} --excluded_list "filtered_reads.txt" \
+                pore-c-aqb digest "${meta.cutter}" --max_monomers ${params.max_monomers} --excluded_list "filtered_reads.txt" \
                 --header "concatemers.bam" \
                 --threads ${digest_annotate_threads} |
             samtools fastq --threads 1 -T '*' |
@@ -75,7 +75,7 @@ process digest_align_annotate {
             """  
         }else{
             """
-            pore-c-py digest "concatemers.bam" "${meta.cutter}" --max_monomers ${params.max_monomers} --excluded_list "filtered_reads.txt" \
+            pore-c-aqb digest "concatemers.bam" "${meta.cutter}" --max_monomers ${params.max_monomers} --excluded_list "filtered_reads.txt" \
                 --header "concatemers.bam" \
                 --threads ${digest_annotate_threads} | 
             samtools fastq --threads 1 -T '*' |
diff --git a/nextflow_schema.json b/nextflow_schema.json
index 57fab6f..9164b06 100644
--- a/nextflow_schema.json
+++ b/nextflow_schema.json
@@ -61,8 +61,8 @@
                 "cutter": {
                     "type": "string",
                     "default": "NlaIII",
-                    "description": "The enzyme used in the restriction digest.",
-                    "help_text": "Any enzyme from the Biopython restriction dictionary can be used. See `https://github.com/biopython/biopython/blob/master/Bio/Restriction/Restriction_Dictionary.py`. This can also be defined per sample: see `--sample_sheet` parameter."
+                    "description": "The enzyme(s) used in the restriction digest.",
+                    "help_text": "Any enzyme from the Biopython restriction dictionary can be used. See `https://github.com/biopython/biopython/blob/master/Bio/Restriction/Restriction_Dictionary.py`. Several enzymes may be given as a comma-separated list, e.g. `DpnII,NlaIII`, in which case fragments are delimited by the union of their recognition sites. This can also be defined per sample: see `--sample_sheet` parameter."
                 }
             },
             "allOf": [
